# Declaration Merging 🧩

If you declare an interface with the **same name multiple times**, TypeScript automatically merges all declarations together!

```typescript
// First declaration
interface WindowCustom {
  title: string;
}

// Second declaration with the exact same name
interface WindowCustom {
  width: number;
  height: number;
}

// Automatically merged! Must have all 3 properties:
const myWin: WindowCustom = {
  title: "Dashboard",
  width: 1024,
  height: 768
};

console.log("Merged Window:", myWin);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
interface WindowCustom {
  title: string;
}
interface WindowCustom {
  width: number;
  height: number;
}

const myWin: WindowCustom = {
  title: "Dashboard",
  width: 1024,
  height: 768
};

console.log("Merged Window:", myWin);
FULL_CODE_END -->

> 💡 **Extending Third-Party Libraries:** Declaration merging is how npm packages allow you to augment external global types (like `Express.Request` or `Window`) with custom properties!

# Type Aliases (`type`) 🏷️

A Type Alias creates a new named reference for any type (primitives, unions, tuples, objects):

```typescript
// 1. Primitive alias
type ID = string | number;

// 2. Union of literals
type Theme = "light" | "dark" | "system";

// 3. Object type
type Coordinates = {
  x: number;
  y: number;
};

const point: Coordinates = { x: 50, y: 100 };
console.log("Coordinates:", point);
```

