# Excess Property Checking 🔍

When assigning an object literal directly to an interface, TypeScript strictly rejects unknown extra properties:

```typescript
interface SimpleUser {
  name: string;
}

// ❌ Error: 'extraField' does not exist in type 'SimpleUser'
// const u: SimpleUser = { name: "Alice", extraField: 123 };

// Workaround: Passing a pre-existing variable bypasses literal check
const rawObj = { name: "Alice", extraField: 123 };
const validUser: SimpleUser = rawObj; // Valid!
console.log("User:", validUser.name);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
interface SimpleUser {
  name: string;
}

const rawObj = { name: "Alice", extraField: 123 };
const validUser: SimpleUser = rawObj;
console.log("User successfully assigned:", validUser.name);
FULL_CODE_END -->

# Generic Interfaces 🧬

Interfaces can take type parameters to handle flexible payloads:

```typescript
interface PaginatedResponse<T> {
  page: number;
  pageSize: number;
  totalItems: number;
  items: T[]; // Array of type T!
}

interface Article {
  id: number;
  title: string;
}

const blogResponse: PaginatedResponse<Article> = {
  page: 1,
  pageSize: 10,
  totalItems: 42,
  items: [
    { id: 1, title: "TypeScript Mastery" },
    { id: 2, title: "CSS Grid Architecture" }
  ]
};

console.log("First Article:", blogResponse.items[0].title);
```

